@@ -5198,6 +5198,7 @@ Resolution rules:
 - before hooks, state publication, branch push, PR lookup/create/update, queue trigger, link persistence, or a success receipt, every mutating reintegration/completion path must replace stale runtime assignment metadata with the authoritative `bead_store_reconciled` assignment set. A branch HEAD whose subject names a bead outside that set is refused with candidate source, canonical status/assignee/duplicate-target evidence, and recovery guidance; closed/deleted/duplicate/superseded/foreign historical bead IDs elsewhere in the landing range are excluded from `Beads:` and lifecycle metadata. An explicit empty authoritative assignment set permits a genuinely beadless operational generation but must never infer bead IDs from goal text, commit bodies, summaries, docs/diff prose, or stale `agent.json` fields (bd-806fb7)
 - a later generation from the same stable agent stream may stack on an open predecessor generation instead of repeating its patch against the integration branch. `caco agent reintegrate --stack-parent <PR-or-generation>` is the explicit CLI/MCP surface; without it Caco may select only the nearest open immutable generation whose exact agent-branch namespace matches and whose fetched head is an ancestor of the child HEAD or whose exact binary patch reverse-applies cleanly to the child tree. Title/bead mentions, substring ownership, cross-agent candidates, provider-blocked/returned parents, and unproven patch similarity must never authorize stacking. The child PR base is the parent immutable head, its diff is therefore incremental, and PR metadata/output exposes the stack base and `blocked_on_parent` state. Reuse deterministically re-applies the desired base; after the parent merges, the same idempotent operation retargets the child to the configured integration base without rewriting the immutable child head or changing its semantic delta (bd-1ce4f9)
 - when a hosted provider returns/rejects/conflicts a PR, retry must remain scoped to the original owner and PR generation. Before mutation, one provider-neutral verifier must require an open PR, durable bead link, exact owner/bead/immutable-generation identity, returned and current head evidence, a real head advance or explicit validated repair receipt, and no competing Track-A/native-auto owner; unchanged head, wrong owner/bead/generation, missing link, closed PR, or ambiguous provider state fail closed with stable reason codes. Block-absent + configured-trigger-present is idempotently already-requeued even while the asynchronously folded provider record is briefly stale (bd-25085f). After the corrected branch is published, the provider adapter must idempotently clear provider-blocking state and restore the configured queue trigger; until that automation exists, profile guidance may require the original owner to run the equivalent provider command (for Aviator: remove `blocked` and add `mergequeue`). No retry path may mutate another owner's PR or arm a competing Track-A/native lander
+- provider-authored PR head updates are classified against exact project/repository/PR/agent/bead/generation identity and retain submitted, previous-provider, observed-provider, and local-generation SHAs plus observation time. Duplicate/equal events are no-ops; stale/out-of-order or cross-identity events are ignored/refused; only a clean local generation proven ancestor of the provider head is fast-forward-ready. Dirty, local-ahead, provider-rewritten, diverged, missing-ref, fetch/auth-failed, or unknown-ancestry cases preserve both sides and return reconciliation-required with a safe first-party next action. An owner that moved to another bead may update only the isolated generation ref/worktree and must never have its current checkout switched/reset (bd-b81dac)
 - the Cacophony project may run one project-scoped persistent `caco-merger` queue operator on its control-plane node. It is a read-mostly controller, not an implementation worker: it must use bounded/event-driven observation, route each actionable return once to the exact original owner or one authoritative successor, acquire the named `caco-merger` lease before queue mutations, reconcile closure only from matching provider/mainline evidence, and file only deduplicated measured optimization work for non-control-plane workers. It must never run application builds, spawn implementation workers on the control-plane node, mutate another owner's head, or race Aviator with Track A/native auto
 - Aviator `mq-bot-*` draft PRs and workflow runs are provider-owned speculative state. Before classifying one as current or stale, queue operations must establish active batch/generation lineage from source PR heads. Current, stale, and ambiguous bot heads/runs must not be cancelled, closed, relabelled, rerun, or pushed by Cacophony; cancelling a current required run can fail every dependent speculative branch. Recovery is limited to routing the affected source PR back to its original owner for requeue after source checks are classified green; no bot-head mutation is permitted
 - the unified merge-queue model must combine local receipts with provider observations using canonical states `published`, `enqueue_failed`, `queued`, `updating`, `checks_pending`, `blocked`, `merged`, `kicked/cancelled`, and `provider_error`; list/API/MCP and UI consumers expose provider, safe public PR link, check summary, blocked reason, age, and action owner, with project/provider/agent/bead filters
